# Categories & Spending Categories

## Valid values

```js
CATEGORIES = [
  'Housing','Transportation','Ride Share','Grocery','Health','Workout',
  'Dining Out','Activity','Gift','Shopping','Travel','Essentials',
  'Work Lunch','Work Coffee','Pre-Tax Health Insurance'
]

SPENDING_CATS = [
  'Necessary Costs','Fun Spending','From Savings','Investments',
  'Saving Goals','Post Tax Income','Pre-Tax','Bonus!'
]
```

Always use these exact strings — casing matters for color lookups and sheet writes.

## Pill color resolution

Category pills use CSS custom properties `--pill` (background) and `--pilltext` (foreground), computed by `getContrastText(hex)` for WCAG contrast.

Resolution order for **category**:
1. `CATEGORY_COLORS` — canonical static map defined inline near the top of `index.html`'s `<script>` block (single source of truth; always wins for known categories)
2. `categoryColors` — live colors fetched from the Google Sheet (fallback for categories not in the static map)
3. `FALLBACK_PILL_COLOR` — `{ bg: '#6b7280' }` (neutral gray, defined alongside `CATEGORY_COLORS`)

Resolution order for **spending category**:
1. `DEFAULT_SPENDING_COLORS` — hardcoded (sheet colors are not fetched for spending cats)
2. `FALLBACK_PILL_COLOR`

## Applying pills in the DOM

Use `getCategorySelectStyle(category)` / `getSpendingSelectStyle(sc)` to get the inline style string, then apply with `applyPill(wrapEl, selectEl, vars)`. Never set pill CSS directly — always go through these helpers so the fallback and contrast logic stays consistent.

## Amazon row coloring (sheet-side)

In `Code.gs`, Amazon rows (`business` contains `'AMAZON'`) always get `bg #fff2cc / font #7d5a00` regardless of category. All other rows inherit the category color from `buildCategoryColorMap`. This is separate from the front-end pill colors.
